{
  "id": "decode-ways",
  "questionId": "decode-ways",
  "slug": "decode-ways",
  "title": "Decode Ways",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "category": "Dp 1d",
  "patterns": [
    "dynamic programming",
    "string"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "blind-75"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "define ways(i) as the number of valid decodings of the first i characters of s - what is ways(0), the empty prefix?",
    "the last decoded group ending at position i-1 is either a single digit or a pair of digits - under what conditions is each of those a legal group?",
    "ways(i) picks up a contribution from ways(i-1) only if s[i-1] is not '0', and a contribution from ways(i-2) only if the two-digit number s[i-2:i] falls between 10 and 26",
    "iterate i from 1 to n, adding both contributions when valid, and roll the last two ways values forward instead of storing a full array"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting that a single '0' is never a valid one-digit group, which silently breaks the recurrence for any string containing a lone 0",
    "allowing two-digit groups starting with '0' (like '05') to count as valid, when only 10-26 are legal",
    "not handling the leading character of the whole string specially when it happens to be '0', which should make the entire answer 0"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A message made only of uppercase letters A through Z was encoded into\ndigits by mapping 'A' -> \"1\", 'B' -> \"2\", ..., 'Z' -> \"26\", producing a\ndigit string `s`.\n\n**Return the number of distinct ways `s` could have been decoded back into letters.**\n\nA digit string with a leading zero in any decoded group is not a valid encoding of anything.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= s.length <= 100",
      "s consists of digits '0' through '9'"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a string that is just '0' (0 ways)",
      "a two-digit group starting with a nonzero digit but exceeding 26, like '27' (only splits as single digits)",
      "a '0' appearing after a digit that can't absorb it, like '90' (0 ways, since '9' alone leaves an orphan leading zero)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"12\"",
        "output": "2",
        "expectedOutput": "2",
        "explanation": "\"12\" could be read as \"1\"+\"2\" (AB) or as \"12\" (L)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"226\"",
        "output": "3",
        "expectedOutput": "3",
        "explanation": "\"2\"+\"2\"+\"6\" (BBF), \"22\"+\"6\" (VF), or \"2\"+\"26\" (BZ)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "s = \"0\"",
        "output": "0",
        "expectedOutput": "0",
        "explanation": "a leading zero can never map to a valid letter, so there are no valid decodings"
      }
    ],
